WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:00.660
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
No matter

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.640
how you use Google
Cloud Platform,

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.910
we have tools to help you get
things done quickly and easily.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.280
In this short video,
we'll cover the Cloud SDK,

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.830
the gcloud command
line, and Cloud Shell.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:19.150
The Cloud Console, our
integrated web-based Google

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.220
Cloud Platform
Management web UI,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.970
is a great place
to start using GCP.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.720
It offers powerful
and centralized site

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:28.630
to manage all of your
resources, projects,

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:29.640
and billing information.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.515
And you should check
out the video link below

00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:32.615
for more details.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:34.240
Next, we'll talk
about the Command Line

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.770
Developer and admin tools.

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.060
Let's start with Cloud SDK.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.050
Cloud SDK UK is a set
of command line tools,

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:44.970
including gloud,
gsutil, and bq, which

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.010
you can use to access
Compute Engine, Cloud

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.740
Storage, BigQuery, and other
products from the command line.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.650
You can run these
tools interactively

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.620
or in your automated scripts.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:58.710
Cloud SDK support on
Linux, Mac OS, and Windows

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:00.570
and requires Python to run.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.390
You can install via
apt-get, or yum,

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.540
or by using an installer in
interactive or non-interactive

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:07.590
mode.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.010
Once installed, the SDK supports
updates and additional features

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:13.380
as we'll see in a minute.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.110
We'll also talk about how not
to install Cloud SDK later

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.580
in this video.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.870
The most used part of
Cloud SDK is gcloud.

00:01:21.870 --> 00:01:24.180
And the typical first
command after installation

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:27.480
is gcloud init to
perform setup tasks.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.120
This takes care of both
authorizing Cloud SDK

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.690
tools to use your user account
credentials to access your GCP

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.200
resources as well as setting
up a configuration, which

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.110
covers the active account,
the current project,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:44.470
and optionally, the default
Compute Engine region and zone.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.470
You can rerun this
command at a later time

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:50.467
to change your settings or to
create a new configuration.

00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.050
You can view the
current configuration

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:56.880
at any time using the
gcloud config list command.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.960
Managing and updating Cloud
SDK is also done using gcloud.

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:03.090
By default, the
SDK installs gcloud

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.505
commands that are
generally available or GA.

00:02:05.505 --> 00:02:08.520
Additionally
functionality is available

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.810
as SDK components
named alpha and beta

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.610
and can be installed using the
gcloud components installed

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.840
command.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.090
Updates to existing
components are achieved

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.600
with gcloud components update.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.100
Once installed and
configured, you

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.760
can achieve virtually
anything with gcloud

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.720
that you can do
with Cloud Console.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.130
In fact, when using
cloud console,

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:33.360
you're often able to obtain
the equivalent gcloud command

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.030
for what you are doing in
the graphical interface.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:42.300
Manage VMs and storage buckets,
setup networking and firewalls,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.500
build and test locally,
deploy to production,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.840
and monitor logs-- it can
all be done with gcloud.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.390
And all of this can be automated
in scripts and [INAUDIBLE] code

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.580
for CI/CD tools.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:57.000
For a richer, more interactive
experience, as you type,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.060
autocompletion is available
via an interactive mode.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.170
Check it out.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.960
It works with gcloud, but also
with the other command line

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:06.810
tools in the SDK.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.460
For those Windows
PowerShell users

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:10.710
out there, do not feel
left out as we have

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:13.200
Cloud Tools for PowerShell.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.560
This set of commandlets
for PowerShell

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.270
will let you manage GCP
resources in a way that

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.490
should feel familiar.

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.920
Let's now switch to Cloud Shell.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.680
Wouldn't it be nice if you
had an always-available,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.710
browser-based
environment with gcloud?

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.320
What if it also came with
all your favorite tools

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:34.260
such as Git, Bash,
build-and-package tools,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:39.390
Docker, kubectl, your language
specific tools, text editors,

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.740
et cetera?

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.020
This is precisely what
Cloud Shell offers,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:46.560
a temporary G1 small
virtual machine,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.440
running a [? DBN ?]
image hosted on GCP

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.780
with all these tools
always up-to-date.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.720
It comes with 5 gigabytes
of persistent disk

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.280
storage for your scripts,
source files, dot files,

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.340
and for your own binaries.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.200
There's even a built in web
editor with support for Java,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:09.150
Go, Python, Node, Ruby,
PHP, and .NET Core--

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.640
all of this from a
simple web browser.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.500
Best of all, it's free.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.440
Cloud shell can be used to
run gcloud commands only

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.829
without having to install,
update, or configure it.

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:24.310
Authorization to GCP projects
and resources is also built-in.

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:27.330
Cloud Shell can also be used
as a development environment

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.480
with its web preview mode,
which enables a browser

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:33.870
to access a web server
running on Cloud Shell--

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:36.450
App Engine's local
SDK for instance.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:40.070
Cloud Shell also has
some advance features.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.130
The boost mode option
will provide you

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.480
with a larger N1 standard
one VM instance for 24 hours,

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:50.620
thus enabling better
CPU and memory.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.290
Cloud uses tmux
by default, which

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:54.790
is great to reconnect
to a running

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:57.720
session in a new browser
window, but also to split

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.260
the window into left and right
panes for debugging purposes.

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.360
Also if you'd like to
customize the environment

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.150
with additional tools, you can
provide your own Docker image.

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.190
A few additional things to know.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:12.230
First, a Cloud Shell
session terminates

00:05:12.230 --> 00:05:14.630
after one hour of inactivity.

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:16.460
Then any modification
that you've

00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:18.180
done outside of
your home directory

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.200
are lost when the
instance is terminated.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.340
Otherwise use the customization
feature that I've mentioned.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:28.430
Finally, if you do not access
Cloud Shell for 120 days,

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.980
your home disk will be deleted.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:33.380
With our iCloud SDK
and Cloud Shell,

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.630
you should have all the
developer and management tools

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:38.720
needed to go build
something awesome.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.740
Ready.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.590
Go.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:43.380
If you'd like this
video, please like

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.930
subscribe, comment,
share, and look forward

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:47.730
to more GCP essentials videos.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:50.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

